Scalers and float LUT textures on GL 2.0 / GLSL 1.20. Three parts:

=== 1 ===
opengl/formats: add GL_ARB_texture_float 16-bit and luminance formats on GL 2.0

Without GL_ARB_texture_rg the GL 2.0 path only registered rgb32f and
rgba32f as float texture formats. Every float LUT with one or two
components therefore had no texture to live in: sh_lut() fell through
to "Can't generate LUT: no compatible methods!" for the 64x64 blue-noise
dither matrix (too large for uniforms), so mpv's default dither silently
degraded to white noise, and the 1-component polar scaler LUT had no
texture either.

GL_ARB_texture_float, which these drivers do advertise, also defines
GL_LUMINANCE(_ALPHA){16,32}F_ARB, so register those as sampleable-only
formats next to rgb16f/rgba16f (uploaded from 32-bit floats like
upstream's formats_float16). The 32-bit luminance variants come first so
LUTs that only need nearest sampling get full precision; they carry no
linear flag since the GPUs of that era (NV4x/G7x, R5xx) filter 16-bit
floats at most. None of them are renderable, matching the existing
rgb(a)32f entries.

On a GeForce 6600 (GL 2.0 NVIDIA-1.5.48) this lets the dither LUT use
a luminance32f texture through the texelFetch() emulation.

=== 2 ===
shaders/sampling: run the direct samplers on GLSL < 130

The bicubic, hermite, gaussian and oversample samplers and the bicubic
distortion mode were disabled on GLSL < 130 because they query the
source size with textureSize(). The texture dimensions are known when
the shader is generated, so on GLSL < 130 pass them in as a vec2 uniform
(sh_tex_size) instead; GLSL >= 130 keeps textureSize(). oversample's
lessThan()/greaterThan() mix() selectors are wrapped in vec2() like the
other GLSL 1.20 fixes.

These samplers take at most four bilinear fetches inside the main pass,
so they cost about as much as bilinear. The renderer substitutes them
for the bicubic/hermite/gaussian filter configs when upscaling a plane,
so e.g. mpv --cscale=bicubic now gets a real bicubic chroma upscale on
GL 2.x instead of silently dropping to bilinear.

The separated (lanczos and friends) and polar scalers stay disabled on
GLSL < 130, now explicitly for performance: they can be emitted for GLSL
1.20 as well, but need extra full-resolution passes per plane, and on
a GeForce 6600 that made 1080p playback tear visibly more. With them off,
mpv's default lanczos keeps falling back to bilinear as before.

=== 3 ===
renderer: route the unavailable scalers on GLSL < 130 to direct sampling

With the separated and polar scalers disabled on GLSL < 130,
dispatch_sampler() learned about it only from the sampler returning
false, logged "Failed dispatching scaler.. disabling" at error level and
set PL_RENDER_ERR_SAMPLING. That flag forces SAMPLER_DIRECT for every
later sampler of the renderer, including the cheap built-in bicubic/
hermite/gaussian replacements: on a GL 2.x context mpv's default
--scale=lanczos would therefore also cancel an explicitly configured
--cscale=bicubic as soon as any luma or chroma scaling went through the
separated path.

Demote SAMPLER_COMPLEX to SAMPLER_DIRECT in sample_src_info() on GLSL <
130, next to the existing no-FBO demotion, and say so once at info level.
The result is the same bilinear fallback as before, without the error
message and without poisoning the other scalers.

